iles | head -50 && ls -la"], ["Bash", "cat events.csv; echo ---; cat README.md; echo ---; cat app.log; echo ---; cat access.log; echo ---; cat crontab.txt; echo ---; ls -R .claude"]], "final_message": "The timestamps in `events.csv` use three different conventions, so the same moment lands on different hours depending on how they're parsed:\n\n```\n1,2026-03-01T10:00:00Z          UTC, explicit\n2,2026-03-01T10:05:00           no offset (naive)\n3,2026-03-01T04:10:00-06:00     UTC-6, explicit\n```\n\n- **Row 3 is 10:10 UTC.** If a chart groups by the clock digits (`04:10`) or drops the offset, that event lands 6 hours early. Rows 1 and 3 are only 10 minutes apart in reality, but they plot 6 hours apart.\n- **Row 2 has no offset, so its meaning depends on the tool.** Pandas leaves it naive. JavaScript's `Date` and many databases treat it as local time. If the machine isn't on UTC, that event shifts by your local offset. If the same data goes through different tools, the shift changes between charts.\n- **Mixing aware and naive values can also change the parse.** In pandas, a column with both can raise an error or fall back to `object` dtype. Some code then coerces or strips the zone silently.\n\nI only have these three rows and no charting code in the repo, so I haven't confirmed which of these is happening in your charts. All three rows would fall within 10 minutes of each other if row 2 is really UTC.\n\n**Fix:**\n1. Parse everything as UTC at load time. In pandas, that's `pd.to_datetime(df.ts, utc=True)`.\n2. Decide what a bare timestamp means and apply it explicitly. If the exporter writes UTC, localize row 2 as UTC. Check with whoever produces the export.\n3. Convert to a display timezone only at plot time.\n4. Better still, have the exporter always emit an offset or `Z`.\n\nIf you share the charting script, I can check where the offset gets lost. I can also write the normalization step.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}